  Nice guys finish last? -- Edison first it is.        //  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/War_of_the_currents

  //  D.C.

  A generator which maintains a constant voltage across itself, once connected to one (or several) conductor(s) becomes a direct current
  (DC) source.
  (Note that a capacitor is not a strict conductor. It would be tempting to refer to it as a semi-conductor, but this expression already refers
  to something distinct. We'll have to accept not-a-conductor as the best label. Do we need labels though?)  
  Assuming everything is constant in the series of conductors, a direct current will have a constant intensity (I = U/R and U and R are constant
  by hypothesis).
  A common example of such generators are lithium batteries.
  Such generators are usually called DC generators even though they don't necessarily generate current (this could explain why brand new lithium
  batteries are usually not empty).

  /*
    Amphibology alert:
      Sometimes DC (direct current) has the looser meaning of "a current drawn by a voltage that is not necessarily constant but whose polarity
      never gets inverted, such that said current never changes direction". (My definition is clumsy because it attempts to have no exception.)

      This understanding of DC could apply, for instance, to a dynamo -- assuming Albert Hofmann somehow cannot focus on constant speed.
      Here, I'll always mean "a current drawn by a constant voltage source".
  */

  Let our points A and B from before become the connectors of a DC generator.
  If we connect an almost ideal cable (R ≈ 0) between A and B, an infinity of electric fields will constantly move from B to A, transferring
  an everlasting constant potential energy to the charge carriers across the cable, turning it into kinetic energy.
  How fast will the charge flow? Well I = U/R --> a constant almost divided by zero. So I is constant and ≈ ∞.
  Easy home experiment: put scissors in the socket (pull a request if you actually did and somehow survived).

  This is called a short-circuit.
  We've mentioned the Joule effect earlier. Look at these formulae: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joule_heating#Direct_current
  This should help admit the need for fuses (usually thin cables intended to melt when I is over a prescribed threshold in order to disconnect
  A and B).

  Ok now let's connect a resistor between A and B.
  R is still constant but no longer negligible. U is still constant. I is still = U/R, that is still constant but no longer near infinite.
  The higher R, the slower I, the more energy converted to heat through the Joule effect. (Variable R would mean variable heat.)
  We've built a radiator!
  This is how toasters operate for instance.

  But we don't need a toaster, do we? Let's unplug the resistor and replace it with a capacitor.
  Let U_gen be the voltage between A and B and U_bd the breakdown voltage of the capacitor.
  If U_gen reaches or exceeds U_bd, the dielectric will become a resistor. It will warm-up and probably burn -- in fact capacitors sometimes
  even explode.
  Yay! We've built a bomb!

  That's all folks. Thanks for reading, like and subscribe.

